    "summary": "Diagnosed and repaired a model-check failure caused by a corrupted instance value.\n\nRoot cause: the instance-layer bearing for support line 1 (Pier 1) carried a stale rubber-layer thickness t1_mm = 8 (and hence te = 7×0.008 = 0.056 m) instead of the description's 11 mm (te = 0.077 m). Although the accepted decisions (S, Kv, Kh) had been computed from the correct geometry, the model builder re-read the corrupted instance value, so the assembled spring properties could not be reconciled with their provenance decisions — failing L2-1 (and cascading to the generic model_checks failure). I re-wrote the instance from the description (all three bearing types confirmed as 11 mm / 5, 7 and 6 layers) and the formulas then reproduced the accepted values exactly (support.1 Kv = 8,029,086 kN/m, Kh = 10,909 kN/m; support.2 Kv = 7,175,049 kN/m).\n\nFinal model (cast-in-place 3-span continuous box girder, 35+45+35 m, spine_beam_2d, continuous_on_piers, C50 box A = 6.712 m², I = 4.498 m⁴, Ec = 37,000 MPa override, γ = 26 kN/m³, w = 174.51 kN/m, m = 17.79 t/m; twin Ø1.8 m C40 piers of 20 m and 27 m; PTFE sliding bearings at abutments and Pier 2, fixed plain elastomeric at Pier 1):\n\nChecks pass: L1 topology (continuous spine, ≥2 vertical supports, longitudinal restraint at Pier 1); L2 code (all decisions recompute from MR-03/MR-04/MR-06/MR-07, MR-00 override, JTG 3362-8.7, JTG D60-4.3.2); L3 mechanics — Σ support reactions = 20,068.9 kN = γ·A·L = 174.512 × 115, and f1 = 3.037 Hz.\n\nKey results: first vertical bending frequency f1 = 3.037 Hz → impact factor μ = 0.1767·ln(f1) − 0.0157 = 0.181 (JTG D60-2015 4.3.2). Self-weight midspan moments 12.5/15.9/12.6 MN·m and support moments −28.5/−28.2 MN·m; governing live-load positive moment 11.47 MN·m and negative moment −9.09 MN·m."
